HIV/AIDS: China reports 14 per cent increase in new cases
China said there has been an increase of 14 per per per cent in the number of people infected with HIV/AIDS.
According to health officials, more than 820,000 people have been affected by the disease.
A total of 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
Most of the new cases are transmitted through sexual intercourse, which marks a change from the past.
HIV has traditionally been rapidly transmitted in some parts of Chinese as a result of a blood translate, using blood transfusion from infected individuals.
China's public health authorities said at a conference in Yunnan province that the number of people infected with HIV in this way has almost disappeared.
However, the number of people infected with HIV and AIDS in China is increasing by one-kin every year.
Influencing HIV through sexual contact between LGBT communities in China is a serious problem.
Homosexuality became a criminal offence in 1997, although discriminations against LGBT people remained harsh.
Due to the conservative values that remain in the country, studies hypocrized 70-90% of men who have sexual intercourse with men will eventually marry women.
Diseases often spread due to non-compliance when men have sex with each other.
As part of its efforts to address this problem since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged to universally make HIV drugs universal.
