World Wide Web to launch a new internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Ley, who invented the World Wide Web, is starting a startup trying to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest project of this technology legend has been built in Solid, Berners-Lee's own open-source platform.
Solid allows users to choose where to sto store the data and which information can be accessed.
In an exclusive interview with the Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the motive behind the intempt was “world dominance”.
"We have to do it right now," he said.
"It's a historic moment."
The app uses Solid's technology to allow people to create their own "Personal Online Data Store" or a POD.
It will consist of contact lists, lists of to-to-do, calendar, music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It's a technology like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook and Slack Spotify are made available at the same time in a single browser.
The speciality of the personal online data store is that it is the user who decides what type of information can be accessed.
The company calls it "personal empowerment through data."
John Bruce, the company's CEO, says the company aims to provide resources, processes, and appropriate skills to provide solid access to everyone.
Berners-Ley and Bruce are a security platform purchased by IBM, and some of the on-staff developers who have been hired to work on the project and a community of volunteer coders.
